WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.993
Embeddings and indexes are sensitive infrastructure in a private RAG system.

2
00:00:04.993 --> 00:00:12.708
An embedding is a mathematical representation used to support similarity search, but it is derived from source content.

3
00:00:12.708 --> 00:00:22.315
When the source material is sensitive, the embedding, nearby metadata, and index records deserve careful treatment as part of the data architecture.

4
00:00:22.315 --> 00:00:31.242
Vector stores may hold embeddings, chunk text, document IDs, tenant labels, permission tags, source references, and lifecycle metadata.

5
00:00:31.242 --> 00:00:33.662
They are not just performance caches.

6
00:00:33.662 --> 00:00:41.378
They are systems that influence which evidence reaches the model and which users can discover source-derived material.

7
00:00:41.378 --> 00:00:48.943
Collection and namespace design should follow data sensitivity, ownership, tenant boundaries, and query patterns.

8
00:00:48.943 --> 00:00:57.642
Mixing unrelated tenants or sensitivity levels into one broad index can make permission enforcement and lifecycle management harder.

9
00:00:57.642 --> 00:01:04.450
Separate collections are not always required, but the design should make access rules clear and testable.

10
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.022
Metadata filters should be deliberate.

11
00:01:07.022 --> 00:01:16.780
Tenant identifiers, classification labels, lifecycle status, source type, effective dates, and permission fields often become retrieval controls.

12
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:26.387
If those fields are incomplete or inconsistent, the retrieval layer may select content that is stale, irrelevant, or inappropriate for the workflow.

13
00:01:26.387 --> 00:01:29.262
Index refresh and deletion behavior matter.

14
00:01:29.262 --> 00:01:34.406
Documents change, permissions move, projects close, and old guidance expires.

15
00:01:34.406 --> 00:01:40.760
The architecture should define how indexes are rebuilt, updated, backed up, restored, and purged.

16
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:50.594
Administrative access to indexes should be protected because index operators may influence retrieval behavior and source-derived data availability.

17
00:01:50.594 --> 00:01:59.142
Teams should document what each index contains, who owns it, how it is refreshed, and what operational signals show that it is healthy.

18
00:01:59.142 --> 00:02:06.782
That record helps reviewers treat the vector store as a managed data system rather than a hidden implementation detail.
